let a panel that is 4:3 to within a percent have the Game Boy body

Upstream draws the body around the picture and gives the shelf slack inside
it, and none of it has ever appeared on this device. Three things said no, and
only one of them was really about this panel:

  - bezel::framed wanted an exactly 4:3 panel. 752x560 is 2256 against 2240,
    sixteen pixels, 0.71% -- off, for a panel nobody would describe as anything
    but 4:3.
  - bezel::pick wanted a bezel asset whose aspect equalled the panel's exactly,
    which refused the only asset upstream ships: 640x480 against 752x560 is
    358,400 to 360,960, the same 0.71%. bezel_face scales the art to the panel
    regardless, so what was being refused was half a percent of stretch on a
    picture of moulded plastic.
  - and the structural one, which no tolerance here could fix: 0002 made the
    canvas the panel exactly, so there was no strip of panel left to draw a
    body on. That is 0002's half of this change -- the canvas is 752x504 now --
    along with painting the thing, since the software backend stored set_bezel
    and never read it.

Both tests here are the same 1/64 window: wide enough for a maker who rounded
560 to a round number, narrow enough that 16:9 and 3:2 stay out and keep
upstream's behaviour on the panels it was written for.

This is a UX change rather than a device fix, which CLAUDE.md asks to be said
out loud: nothing was broken, the frame is simply a thing this port did not
have. It costs 56 rows of picture, and was built to be looked at and judged
rather than argued about.

